{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: The substitution is silent — warn when a numeric project is replaced**

Before this change a numeric "project" failed loudly (ADO `TF200016: The following project does not exist: 879`). Now the `jira.project` tier can point at an ADO project with a *different* name than the tracker's real project, and the work item is silently created there (parent links still resolve cross-project, so nothing errors). A one-line `console.warn` keeps the substitution observable in job logs without changing behavior:

```js
function adoProjectFor(project) {
    var p = String(project == null ? '' : project).trim();
    if (p && !/^\d+$/.test(p)) return p;
    var resolved = (config && config.tracker && config.tracker.adoProject) ||
        (config && config.jira && config.jira.project) ||
        _adoProjectEnv() ||
        p;
    if (p !== resolved) {
        console.warn('trackers: ADO project "' + p + '" is not a project name — using "' + resolved + '" (config.tracker.adoProject / config.jira.project / ADO_PROJECT)');
    }
    return resolved;
}
```

GraalJS-safe (`var`, string concat). Also worth noting in the comment: an all-digit ADO project name is indistinguishable from an id — deployments that have one must set `config.tracker.adoProject` explicitly (the pass-through escape hatch already exists since nothing-configured keeps `p`).
